    "q-28968": {
      "dom_id": "step-q28968",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=(0.6)^{-1}=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,\\frac{5}{3}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/0c5bf6531bf352da158771f92eaab5a3974ccd623d1bf29dbe3ecba287f61d5d.png)",
