    "q-47747": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47747",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $\n\nII. It's a decreasing function\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/288adf3d1da5cf4bb6e2530318aeb083b69d43639de7704f9df15672be477d0a.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
