    "q-47731": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47731",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
