      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
