    "q-7246": {
      "dom_id": "step-q7246",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/f960cca1134d756cded381a2236dd427efb928b1081254173e4c6903b6de150e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
